      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: tighten findSchema.limit from Type.Number to Type.Integer with execution guard",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n**Problem**: `findSchema.limit` accepts floating-point values via `Type.Number()`, but \"Max results\" is inherently an integer-valued field. Float values like `limit=5.5` silently pass `Type.Number` validation and reach tool execution with ambiguous behavior.\n\n**Solution**: Tighten `limit` to `Type.Integer()` with a runtime `Number.isSafeInteger` execution guard. **Per ClawSweeper review, `minimum: 1` has been removed** — zero and negative values retain the existing runtime normalization behavior, so the change only rejects fractional (non-integer) values.\n\n**What changed / NOT changed**:\n1. Schema: `Type.Number()` → `Type.Integer()` (no minimum constraint)\n2. Runtime: `Number.isSafeInteger(limit)` guard rejects non-integer at execution boundary\n3. Focused regression tests (10 cases): valid int, float rejection, zero/negative acceptance (preserved normalization), optional, required pattern, execution boundary\n4. Root proof script removed per ClawSweeper review\n\nThe runtime `Number.isSafeInteger` guard rejects fractional values at the tool-call boundary. `normalizePositiveLimit` handles value clamping. Zero and negative values continue to be normalized at runtime (existing behavior preserved).\n\n**merge-risk declaration**: Low — schema + guard change to internal tool parameter. Only fractional values are rejected; zero and negative values retain existing runtime normalization. No session state, auth, message delivery, or security boundary involved.\n\n## Tests and validation\n\n- `pnpm build:ci-artifacts` → exit 0 ✓\n- Focused regression: 10/10 passed ✓\n  - Schema layer: valid int, float rejection, zero/negative acceptance, optional, required pattern\n  - Execution boundary: float rejection, valid integer no-rejection\n  - All tests validate against actual production schema from `createFindToolDefinition`\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] Backwards compatible — positive integer callers work identically; fractional callers get clear validation error; zero/negative values retain existing normalization\n- [x] Tested with existing configurations\n- [x] New regression tests added\n\n/cc @openclaw/clawsweeper",
